WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.016
Completion-style prompts and chat-style prompts are related but not identical.

00:00:05.016 --> 00:00:09.968
A completion prompt asks the model to continue or respond to a block of text.

00:00:09.968 --> 00:00:14.341
A chat prompt usually has roles such as system, user, and assistant.

00:00:14.341 --> 00:00:26.367
Many instruction-tuned models are trained with specific structures that tell the model where instructions begin, where user content appears, and where the assistant response should start.

00:00:26.367 --> 00:00:28.876
Chat templates encode those structures.

00:00:28.876 --> 00:00:33.892
They may include special tokens, role markers, separators, or stop conditions.

00:00:33.892 --> 00:00:37.558
Different model families can expect different formatting.

00:00:37.558 --> 00:00:45.018
If the wrong template is used, a capable model may produce weak, oddly structured, or instruction-misaligned output.

00:00:45.018 --> 00:00:51.127
It may repeat role labels, ignore system guidance, continue the user's text, or stop too early.

00:00:51.127 --> 00:00:54.343
Tokenizer compatibility is part of the same issue.

00:00:54.343 --> 00:01:00.452
The model was trained to interpret text through a specific tokenizer and set of special tokens.

00:01:00.452 --> 00:01:05.018
Prompt formatting should match that expectation as closely as possible.

00:01:05.018 --> 00:01:14.536
A runtime or web UI may apply templates automatically, but users still need to know when formatting is part of the problem they are troubleshooting.

00:01:14.536 --> 00:01:18.073
Test prompts consistently before judging model quality.

00:01:18.073 --> 00:01:25.212
Use the same model, quantization, context length, sampling settings, and chat template when comparing behavior.

00:01:25.212 --> 00:01:29.971
If the output is poor, verify formatting before assuming the model is bad.

00:01:29.971 --> 00:01:36.916
In local inference, many quality problems are actually configuration problems wearing a convincing disguise.
